90
00:06:56,080 --> 00:06:58,940
This is an effective overview for mixed charts.

91
00:06:59,800 --> 00:07:05,060
With this type, I still write two sentences in the overview, including

92
00:07:05,060 --> 00:07:10,700
global trend plus global feature if there are charts with trends, or

93
00:07:10,700 --> 00:07:14,100
two global features if both are charts of comparison.

94
00:07:15,080 --> 00:07:20,160
These two sentences can start with expressions such as, it is clear

95
00:07:20,160 --> 00:07:25,540
from the pie chart that, and it can also be seen from the table that.

96
00:07:25,540 --> 00:07:30,800
Now, let's move to the major three and the body paragraphs.

97
00:07:35,640 --> 00:07:40,840
Please spend 10 minutes analysing the body based on three questions below,

98
00:07:40,840 --> 00:07:45,220
corresponding to the three elements of the major three.

99
00:07:46,220 --> 00:07:51,340
Grouping information, how is the information divided into two body paragraphs?

100
00:07:52,440 --> 00:07:57,240
Individual features, which details and statistics are selected?

101
00:07:58,220 --> 00:08:02,160
Detail organisation, how is the information organised?

102
00:08:04,930 --> 00:08:09,830
To answer the first question, grouping information, how is the information

103
00:08:09,830 --> 00:08:13,750
divided into two body paragraphs, I think many of you will be

104
00:08:13,750 --> 00:08:16,010
surprised by how simple the answer is.

105
00:08:16,910 --> 00:08:22,110
We have two distinct charts, so we describe each chart in one body paragraph.

106
00:08:22,110 --> 00:08:28,110
That's right, each paragraph focuses on one chart, so there's no need

107
00:08:28,110 --> 00:08:30,050
to compare or connect them directly.

108
00:08:30,850 --> 00:08:35,830
This actually makes mixed charts simpler than single charts because each one

109
00:08:35,830 --> 00:08:37,669
has less information to process.

110
00:08:38,789 --> 00:08:45,030
Therefore, if you see a mixed chart in your exam, be confident, it is not difficult at all.

111
00:08:45,030 --> 00:08:47,350
Handle it just as we've learned today.

112
00:08:48,070 --> 00:08:51,270
A quick note, if you want to achieve a high score in

113
00:08:51,270 --> 00:08:56,010
coherence and cohesion, you must start each body paragraph with a cohesive

114
00:08:56,010 --> 00:08:59,250
device to signal which chart you are talking about.

115
00:08:59,970 --> 00:09:04,750
The two most effective ways, as used in this sample, are from

116
00:09:04,750 --> 00:09:08,470
the pie chart or in terms of the pie chart.

117
00:09:09,430 --> 00:09:14,770
Remember, in terms of plus noun means you are talking about a specific object.

118
00:09:14,770 --> 00:09:18,690
And for the second body, regarding the table.

119
00:09:19,550 --> 00:09:25,170
Regarding with regard to or in regard to all mean the same thing.

120
00:09:25,170 --> 00:09:32,370
Moving on to question two, individual features, which details and statistics are selected.

121
00:09:32,370 --> 00:09:37,110
We see that all the chosen details are the highest, like the

122
00:09:37,110 --> 00:09:44,610
Australians, the lowest, the Dutch, and significant comparisons, like the urban Chinese population.

123
00:09:45,550 --> 00:09:49,050
This is similar to what we did with trend and comparison charts.

124
00:09:50,050 --> 00:09:56,130
As for question three, detail organisation, we still follow the same golden rule.

125
00:09:56,130 --> 00:10:00,090
Describe from the most significant to the least significant.

126
00:10:00,950 --> 00:10:06,210
Present the most important information first and then the less important ones.

127
00:10:06,210 --> 00:10:20,000
We've analysed a band six plus sample with quite good task achievement and coherence and cohesion.

128
00:10:20,700 --> 00:10:25,100
By now, I'm sure you don't find mixed charts scary anymore.

129
00:10:25,700 --> 00:10:29,820
They even seem easier to deal with compared to charts with trends

130
00:10:29,820 --> 00:10:33,140
or charts of comparison we learned in previous lessons.

131
00:10:34,160 --> 00:10:36,000
Let me summarise the knowledge for you.

132
00:10:36,820 --> 00:10:41,620
The three analysis steps are similar to what we do with other chart types.

133
00:10:41,620 --> 00:10:45,300
The big three, the great three, the major three.

134
00:10:46,400 --> 00:10:51,260
Please note that when writing the introduction, if you need to use

135
00:10:51,260 --> 00:10:58,160
two clauses to introduce each chart, you can connect them into one sentence using while or and.

136
00:10:59,140 --> 00:11:06,120
The overview still includes two sentences, global trend and global feature or

137
00:11:06,120 --> 00:11:09,120
two global features if there's no trend.

138
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:13,020
We can use each overview sentence for one chart.

139
00:11:14,200 --> 00:11:19,600
Lastly, when writing the body, we describe each chart in a separate paragraph.

140
00:11:19,600 --> 00:11:24,200
There's no need to connect the two charts and it's important to

141
00:11:24,200 --> 00:11:29,300
use cohesive devices to show readers which chart you're discussing, such as

142
00:11:29,300 --> 00:11:34,680
in terms of or regarding with in regard to.

143
00:11:34,680 --> 00:11:41,900
A type of question that used to be a nightmare for IELTS takers now becomes so easy.

144
00:11:42,760 --> 00:11:46,520
Maybe many of you here even hope for mixed charts in the test room.

145
